  "reading": "With disjoint keys the insertion step's cost per record does not grow with the number of nodes: the median over the last decile of the stream is 48.58 microseconds at K = 8 and 44.98 microseconds at K = 128, although the whole-stream mean rises from 55.17 to 227.52 microseconds, because minting the nodes is itself what costs. With shared keys, where every node supports every key and candidate retrieval returns the whole graph, the claim fails: the same statistic is 161.48 microseconds at 1 node(s), 113.0 at 5 and 349.17 at 20, and at planted K in [32, 128] the repair pass did not finish the stream inside the 180 second budget. Cost per record is flat in stream length (33.37 microseconds at n = 3000, 35.08 at n = 100000, about 22000 records per second for the insertion step alone), but the repair pass grows superlinearly and takes 95.8 percent of wall time at n = 100000, which is what cuts end-to-end throughput to 908.8 records per second; peak resident set is 242.45 MB there, read as bytes because this run was on darwin."
}